<div class="wrap">
<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">db_route · research brief · 13 Sep 2026</div>
  <h1>Is that all the data?</h1>
  <p class="lede">What OpenTSLM actually did with its data, what the frontier time-series models do, which of it transfers to a 15,000-record rail fine-tune, and where the next data really comes from.</p>
</header>

<div class="answer">
  <div class="eyebrow">Short answer</div>
  <p><strong>No — but the next data is not more months.</strong> Every published result that applies to our regime says the same three things:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Breadth beats depth, 12.5× per unit of work.</strong> A fresh <code>(service, station)</code> entity is worth 2.78 effective samples; a sixth anchor on an existing one is worth 0.22. Any augmented copy is worth less than that. Widening the catchment (done: 2,691 stations, six regions) was the right move; synthetic data is not.</li>
    <li><strong>The pretrained corpus reaches us through encoder weights, not our pipeline.</strong> OpenTSLM's own Table 3: swapping the from-scratch encoder for Chronos-2 lifted Sleep-CoT F1 from 51.4 → 68.3 on Gemma-270M. No augmentation anywhere in the literature produces a gain that size.</li>
    <li><strong>The highest-value external data is internal.</strong> Ranked by hours-to-gain, the top three items need no new source at all: upstream-delay features from the run sequence, a timetable-sparsity channel, and a station join hub. Weather is worth a thin version for seven cause classes only; crowding and concerts are dead ends.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h2>What OpenTSLM actually did</h2>
<div class="verdict"><strong>Verdict.</strong> The repo contains no data-construction code — every loader downloads a finished CSV from an ETH polybox share. The recipe was recovered by downloading all four releases and measuring them, and it is less careful than the paper implies.</div>

<p>The paper says <em>"we release all code, datasets, and models."</em> The datasets are downloadable; the merge, resampling, windowing, plot rendering and GPT-4o calls that made them were never published. What can be measured from the files:</p>

<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Source</th><th class="num">Train rows</th><th>Split policy</th><th>What the audit found</th></tr>
<tr><td>TSQA</td><td>Third-party HF (<code>ChengsenWang/TSQA</code>), fully synthetic</td><td class="num">38,400</td><td>random, seed 42</td><td>Not theirs; 4 tasks × 3 labels × 4,000, perfectly balanced</td></tr>
<tr><td>M4-Captions</td><td>M4 series + GPT-4o captions</td><td class="num">80,000</td><td>random</td><td><span class="bad">706 captions are literal API error strings</span>; 99.3% describe the plot, not the series</td></tr>
<tr><td>HAR-CoT</td><td>8 accelerometer corpora merged offline</td><td class="num">68,542</td><td>pre-baked, unrecorded</td><td>No provenance column at all — cannot be re-split by subject; 138 windows verbatim in both train and test</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleep-CoT</td><td>SleepEDF, <strong>6 recordings</strong></td><td class="num">7,434</td><td>stratified by label at load</td><td>Every test subject is in train; paper says 5 classes, file has 6; paper says 100 Hz, file is 50 Hz</td></tr>
<tr><td>ECG-QA-CoT</td><td>ECG-QA templates × PTB-XL</td><td class="num">159,313</td><td>official record-level folds</td><td>The one clean split. <span class="bad">2,536 rationales (1.6%) end with the wrong answer</span> despite the label being printed in the prompt</td></tr>
</table></div>

<p><strong>Augmentation: none.</strong> Zero hits across 163 files for jitter, warp, mixup, crop, flip, synthetic or noise. <code>QADataset</code> materialises every sample once into a class-level list; every epoch sees byte-identical tensors. <strong>Curriculum: five strictly sequential stages, no mixing, no replay, no ablation anywhere in the paper.</strong> Their own §4.6 shows a pretrained Chronos-2 encoder beats the two warm-up stages it was meant to replace.</p>

<div class="grid2">
  <div class="card"><h4>Copy</h4><ul>
    <li>Per-sample z-score, then re-inject mean and std as text — it is what lets incompatible sources share one encoder (our bridge already does this).</li>
    <li>Store the generation prompt as a column in every row; it is the only reason their recipe could be audited.</li>
    <li>The <code>Answer: &lt;label&gt;</code> terminator: it makes format failures countable.</li>
    <li><code>PAMAP2Dataset._make_windows</code>: group by subject so no window spans two, drop windows whose modal label covers &lt; 50%. They wrote it and never used it.</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="card"><h4>Avoid</h4><ul>
    <li>Dropping provenance. HAR-CoT is permanently untraceable to its eight sources.</li>
    <li>Random-splitting windows from a handful of entities (Sleep-CoT). Split by station and by date — ours does.</li>
    <li>Shipping generated text without a validator. A ten-line filter removes all 706 error captions and all 2,536 wrong answers.</li>
    <li>A sequential curriculum with no replay and no stage-skipping ablation.</li>
  </ul></div>
</div>

<h2>What the frontier models do</h2>
<div class="verdict"><strong>Verdict.</strong> They solve a pretraining problem we don't have. The direction of travel is <em>more synthetic data at pretraining</em>; nobody has published evidence that synthetic data helps a single-domain fine-tune, and the one survey that looks (Salesforce, arXiv 2503.11411) lists it as an open problem.</div>

<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Model</th><th>Real corpus</th><th>Synthetic / augmentation</th><th>What their ablation actually measured</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chronos (Amazon)</td><td>28 datasets, ~890k series, ~84B obs</td><td>TSMixup (K=3, Dirichlet weights) + KernelSynth (GP kernel bank, 1M series), mixed <strong>9:1</strong></td><td>In-domain gain from TSMixup: <strong>nil</strong> — only zero-shot improved. Synthetic helps up to ~10%, then <em>hurts</em>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chronos-2</td><td>19 datasets</td><td>Multivariate/covariate data is <strong>100% synthetic</strong></td><td>Synthetic-only model is "only slightly below" the full one. "Real data may not even be required for effective pretraining."</td></tr>
<tr><td>MOIRAI / LOTSA (Salesforce)</td><td>27B obs, ten archives, hand-deduplicated at dataset level</td><td>None; ε=0.001 per-dataset sampling cap for balance</td><td>"w/o LOTSA" costs +23.5% MAE, attributed to diversity — but the experiment cannot separate diversity from volume.</td></tr>
<tr><td>TimesFM (Google)</td><td>~100B points incl. Google Trends + Wiki pageviews</td><td>Synthetic for <em>frequency coverage</em></td><td>"Almost no difference" on hourly data, where the granularity was already well represented.</td></tr>
<tr><td>MOMENT (CMU)</td><td>Time Series Pile, 13 domains</td><td>None</td><td>No data-scaling result. The paper's real contribution is the scarcity argument.</td></tr>
<tr><td>TTM (IBM)</td><td>LibCity slice of LOTSA + Monash, ~1B</td><td>Resolution resampling (DRS)</td><td>The one clean number: resampling existing series to new resolutions = <strong>+30%</strong>; adding a whole new archive (4× data) = <strong>+6%</strong>.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h3>Why TSMixup and KernelSynth don't transfer here</h3>
<p>Three independent reasons, each from the source paper. <strong>Chronos measured the in-domain effect and it was nil</strong> — our evaluation is entirely in-domain. <strong>The mechanism samples a dataset index uniformly from 28 datasets</strong>; with one archive it degenerates into averaging pairs of German delay series, a smoothed old pattern rather than a new one. <strong>It breaks the label</strong>: our targets are functions of <code>(entity, anchor)</code>; a convex mix of two histories has no coherent horizon, no <code>ctx_*</code> slots and no defensible rationale. KernelSynth's measured job was frequency coverage — we control our resolutions, so there is nothing for GP samples to fill.</p>

<p>Two augmentations that the largest independent benchmark (Iwana &amp; Uchida, 128 UCR datasets × 6 models) says will actively hurt: <strong>permutation</strong> (5/6 models degraded — destroys the weekly structure L/XM exist to capture) and <strong>flipping</strong> (6/6 degraded, 5 significantly — a negated delay is physically meaningless). Time warping degraded 5/6. Across 72 cells, 61% of augmentation effects were negative.</p>

<h2>What does transfer</h2>
<div class="verdict"><strong>Verdict.</strong> Our model is an LLM with a LoRA adapter, so the governing literature is instruction-tuning, not pretraining corpora. LIMA's result — 1,000 curated examples, and a 16× increase in data produced no improvement — is the scaling law for our regime.</div>

