<h3>Marginal value of the next record, from our own design effect</h3>
<p>With intra-entity correlation ρ ≈ 0.20, the M-th anchor on an entity is worth <code>M/(1+(M−1)ρ)</code> effective samples cumulatively. The 15,000 nominal records are ~8,300 effective. What one more of each is worth:</p>
<div class="bars">
  <span>A fresh entity</span><div class="bar" style="width:100%"></div><span class="val">2.78</span>
  <span>6th anchor, existing entity</span><div class="bar" style="width:8%"></div><span class="val">0.22</span>
  <span>10th anchor</span><div class="bar" style="width:4%"></div><span class="val">0.11</span>
  <span>Augmented copy (ρ ≈ 1)</span><div class="bar dim" style="width:2%"></div><span class="val">&lt; 0.05</span>
</div>

<h3>The ranked list</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Finding</th><th>Evidence</th><th>What it means for us</th></tr>
<tr><td>Entity breadth over anchor depth</td><td>Design effect above; Moirai's ε-cap is the same instrument</td><td>Keep the curation quota (≥100 stations, ≤40% one train type). Six regions was worth more than 16 extra months.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pretrained encoder over from-scratch</td><td>OpenTSLM Table 3: 51.4 → 68.3 F1 (Gemma-270M, Sleep-CoT)</td><td>Flamingo + Chronos-2 encoder is the single largest lever available. Caveat: Chronos mean-scales to ±15×|mean|; our bounded share channels need standardisation instead.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data plateaus early for LoRA on an LLM</td><td>LIMA §5: no gain across a 16× size increase; quality and prompt diversity did move the needle</td><td>At 15k the binding constraint is epochs-to-overfit. Early stopping and LoRA rank, not more rows.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Their own smallest dataset worked</td><td>Sleep-CoT: 7,434 train rows produced the paper's best F1</td><td>We are at 2× that with cleaner splits.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pretrained advantage erodes with data and epochs</td><td>TTM Table 7: worth 36% zero-shot, 21% at 5% data, 2% at 10% data / 50 epochs</td><td>If S/M/L/XM converge in the bake-off, that is the expected shape.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Above every measured break-even</td><td>arXiv 2607.04919: classical methods overtake FMs at 21–8,361 samples on half of 30 datasets; RIDE (94.5M rail events): XGBoost within 4% of the best GNN</td><td><strong>Run a gradient-boosting baseline on the <code>ctx_*</code> scalars before the pitch.</strong> A jury will ask.</td></tr>
<tr><td>If augmenting at all: frequency domain</td><td>Lag-Llama shipped Freq-Mix/Freq-Mask (p=0.5); window slicing recommended by Iwana &amp; Uchida</td><td>Window slicing is what the S/M/L/XM ladder already does. Report the ladder as the augmentation study.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stop exact duplication</td><td>Lee et al. 2021: deduplicated LMs memorise 10× less</td><td><code>REPEAT_MAX = 4</code> means 800 exposures of one rationale over 200 epochs. Cap at 2 or move to a per-sample loss weight.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h2>Where more data actually comes from</h2>
<div class="verdict"><strong>Verdict.</strong> The top three items need no external source. Hours are for one person with the corpus already loaded; the ranking held up under a second agent's independent check.</div>

<p>First, the premise: the 10 months we hold <em>is</em> the full-coverage era. The archive runs from 2024-07, but before 2025-11-02 it holds ~107 stations, after it ~5,300. Concatenating naively puts a station-coverage regime change in the middle of every long context, which is why the anchor window now starts 120 days after the expansion. The 16 older months add winter 2024/25 at the hubs only — worth it if we specifically want a second winter, not otherwise.</p>

<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Hours</th><th>Expected gain</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Station join hub</strong> — <code>db-stations</code> full.json (ISC, 5,388 stations): EVA → lat/lon, DS100, Bundesland, category. Unlocks weather, infra, holidays and a footfall proxy in one file.</td><td class="num">0.75</td><td>Enables everything below</td><td><span class="pill do">do first</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Upstream-delay features from own data</strong> — delay at stops n−1, n−2 of the same run; previous train at this station; rolling 60-min station median; scheduled dwell and slack. Dutch GATv2 study: <code>lag2_delay</code> importance 74× the next feature.</td><td class="num">1–1.5</td><td>Largest single gain available</td><td><span class="pill do">do</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Timetable-sparsity channel</strong> — planned stops per station-hour ÷ 28-day rolling median. DB loads emergency and construction timetables ahead of time, so this one column captures strikes, storms and closures with no external feed.</td><td class="num">0.5</td><td>Free proxy for four cause families</td><td><span class="pill do">do</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Calendar</strong> — OpenHolidays API per Bundesland (7 of 16 public holidays differ across our six regions; 358 days of cross-region school-holiday contrast). Encode <code>days_to_holiday_start</code> clipped ±3, plus the 2025-12-14 Fahrplanwechsel.</td><td class="num">0.75</td><td>Small, real, cross-region variance</td><td><span class="pill do">do</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>DB InfraGO Infrastrukturdaten</strong> — CC-BY 4.0 plain download (27.9 MB): track count, electrification, line speed, <code>Bauzustand = im Bau</code>, plus the legally-declared congested-line lists (26 ÜLS). Joins via DS100 → Streckennummer + km.</td><td class="num">2–3</td><td>Best external dataset that exists; feeds ~46% of cause mass</td><td><span class="pill maybe">if time</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Weather via Bright Sky (DWD)</strong> — free, hourly, historical. Central-German DML study: ~0 effect on delay <em>magnitude</em>; real only for the seven weather cause codes.</td><td class="num">1–1.5</td><td>Cause-head only. Check the weather-class base rate first; if &lt; 1%, skip</td><td><span class="pill maybe">thin version</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Zensus 2022 100 m population grid</strong> — free; catchment population within N m of each station as a static demand feature.</td><td class="num">1</td><td>Worth more than any event source</td><td><span class="pill maybe">if time</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Football fixtures (openligadb) — 154 of 306 Bundesliga matches kick off Saturday 15:30, collinear with features we have; tags 0.1% of rows; Allianz Arena has no DB station.</td><td class="num">1</td><td>~0.1 s MAE; demo anecdote</td><td><span class="pill no">slide only</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Crowding, concerts, trade fairs, strikes, Oktoberfest, bahn.de load factor, Träwelling</td><td class="num">—</td><td>No API, no history, or booking forecasts blocked from datacenter IPs</td><td><span class="pill no">dead</span></td></tr>
</table></div>

<p>Two label-hygiene findings from the same research that are worth more than any dataset: IRIS codes 69–99 are comfort messages (WLAN, missing first class), not delay causes — our <code>causes.py</code> already excludes them; and code 23 <em>Betriebsstabilisierung</em> was deleted on 2025-12-14, a taxonomy change inside our window.</p>

<h2>The one thing to steal</h2>
<div class="verdict"><strong>Verdict.</strong> Not an augmentation. A two-dimensional held-out grid, with the gap between cells published — and we already run it.</div>

<p>arXiv 2510.13654 §3.2: for globally-trained models a correct temporal split is <em>not sufficient</em>. If test entities share a time period with training entities that are causally coupled, the model learns the shock from one and reproduces it on the other. Measured: <strong>43.5% MAE</strong> in a Madrid transport experiment structurally identical to ours, and up to 37% with only 0.78% temporal overlap. German rail is the worst case — knock-on propagation is not a confounder we might have, it is a channel we compute.</p>

<p><code>plan_anchors(split_mode="both")</code> emits exactly the four cells this calls for, and it is what every measurement today ran with. Cell C (unseen entity, later date; 651 anchors, 409 entities) is the production number; the A − C gap is the memorisation estimate. Expect cell B (unseen entity, shared dates) to look optimistic and say why in the deck: indirect leakage through network-wide shocks, measured at 37–43% elsewhere.</p>

<h2>What changed in the pipeline today</h2>
<div class="verdict"><strong>Verdict.</strong> Record yield went from 9 of 180 to 148 of 150. None of it was a data gap; all four causes were accounting errors about what an empty bucket means.</div>

<div class="kpi">
  <div><b>5%</b><span>usable records, before</span></div>
  <div><b>99%</b><span>usable records, after (S and XM)</span></div>
  <div><b>0</b><span>changes to the fact cache or the source data</span></div>
</div>

<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Cause</th><th>Measured</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cause-share channels were <em>interpolated</em> across buckets with no attributed delay</td><td>63 of 146 rejections; median 0.22 imputed at station grain</td><td>Zero-imputed. "No delay blamed on works" is a fact, not a gap; <code>attributed_share</code> keeps zero unambiguous.</td></tr>
<tr><td>One denominator (the station's open hours) judged every grain</td><td>45 of 146; a service not due this hour counted as a service-channel gap</td><td>Per-grain count channels: <code>station_throughput</code>, new <code>service_throughput</code>, pair = both intersected.</td></tr>
<tr><td>XM's coarse layers had no count channel at all</td><td>91 of 150 XM windows; every quarter/season gap judged against raw step count</td><td>Both count channels ride every layer of every profile.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anchors from Nov–Jan reached back past the 2025-11-02 coverage expansion</td><td>44 of 44 rejected</td><td>Anchor window starts 120 days after the archive expands, for all profiles — the "L-limited shared anchor set" the plan already required.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Bus</code> qualified as a train entity</td><td>336k events/month; one curated bus ran 1, 3 and 6 times in three sampled weeks</td><td>Rail-replacement excluded as an entity; still counted in station throughput as evidence a line was shut.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<p>Profile shapes after the change: S 7 series / 294 patches, M 10 / 600, L 10 / 300, XM 19 / 834 — all under OpenTSLM's 1,024-patch cap. <code>service_at_station_delay</code> now lives only at the 6-hourly and daily layers; one service at one station is ~93% empty at hourly resolution by timetable, not by data loss.</p>

